<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1841 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Deutschlandlied</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="de">Das Lied der Deutschen</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A poem of 1841 calling for a united Germany, sung to a tune by Joseph Haydn; its third verse is Germany's national anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>August Heinrich Hoffmann von Fallersleben</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Joseph Haydn</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German liberal nationalists of the 19th century; later the German state</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Heligoland (then British, now Germany)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1841; melody 1797</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by August Heinrich Hoffmann von Fallersleben (1841); music by Joseph Haydn (1797))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The melody is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a>'s, written in 1797 for Lorenz Leopold Haschka's &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a>&quot;, a birthday anthem for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Emperor Francis II</a> modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;. It remained the anthem of the Austrian emperors, with new words from 1854, until the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in 1918.</p><p>In 1841, while on holiday on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> island of Heligoland, then British, the poet and linguist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-von-fallersleben">August Heinrich Hoffmann von Fallersleben</a> wrote &quot;Das Lied der Deutschen&quot; as a new text for Haydn's tune. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was then divided among the many states of the German Confederation, and after the Congress of Vienna and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees">Carlsbad Decrees</a> of 1819 Metternich's police censored universities and pursued liberals. The poem's first stanza was an appeal to the German princes to put a united Germany before the independence of their small states, and its third stanza asked for a united and free Germany under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a> rather than arbitrary monarchy; at the time this was a revolutionary idea. The next year Hoffmann lost his post as librarian and professor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a> because of this and other works, and lived in hiding until he was pardoned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a>.</p><p>The song was first sung at an official ceremony in 1890, on Heligoland, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heligoland-zanzibar-treaty">Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty</a> made the island German, and it became one of the best-known patriotic songs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. It became very popular after the Battle of Langemarck in 1914, when German regiments made up mostly of young students reportedly attacked the British lines singing it and suffered heavy losses. By then critics abroad read its first line as a claim of German supremacy over other peoples, a meaning its author had not intended.</p><p>On 11 August 1922 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a>, a Social Democrat, made the whole song the anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, as a political trade-off for the conservative right after the black, red and gold flag had been adopted in 1919, though he favoured singing the third stanza only. Under the Nazi regime (1933–1945) only the first stanza was sung, followed by the party's &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a>&quot;; three thousand Germans sang it as Hitler entered the stadium at the opening of the 1936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1936-summer-olympics">Berlin Olympics</a>. That stanza became closely identified with the Nazi regime.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> had no anthem at first. In 1952, at Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a>'s request, President Theodor Heuss accepted the song as the national anthem, with only the third stanza sung on official occasions; the other stanzas were not outlawed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> had its own anthem, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a>&quot;. In 1990 the Federal Constitutional Court ruled that only the third stanza is protected by criminal law as the anthem, and in November 1991 President Richard von Weizsäcker and Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl">Helmut Kohl</a> agreed in an exchange of letters that the third stanza alone is the anthem of reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The opening words of that stanza are an unofficial national motto, found on Bundeswehr belt buckles and German 2 euro coins.</p><p>The first stanza is not illegal, but singing it is taboo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and its list of rivers and a strait as borders, none of which lies in Germany today, has been criticised as irredentist. Friedrich Nietzsche called its claim the most idiotic slogan in the world, and Kurt Tucholsky named a satirical photo book of 1929 after it. It has more than once been played or sung by mistake abroad: German athletes were appalled when it was played at the canoe sprint world championships in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 2011, and at a Fed Cup match in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> in 2017 German players and fans sang the third stanza to drown out the soloist.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic poem in three stanzas: the first places <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> above loyalty to its many separate states, the second is a toast in the manner of a drinking song, and the third, the anthem of today, asks for a united and free Germany under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>. The mood is earnest and stately, carried by Haydn's hymn tune.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Deutschland, Deutschland über Alles,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Germany, Germany above all,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Über Alles in der Welt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Above all in the world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wenn es stets zu Schutz und Trutze</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When, for protection and defiance, it always</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Brüderlich zusammenhält.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Holds together like brothers.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Von der Maas bis an die Memel,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the Meuse to the Memel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Von der Etsch bis an den Belt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the Adige to the Belt,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Deutschland, Deutschland über Alles,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Germany, Germany above all,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Über Alles in der Welt!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Above all in the world!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Deutsche Frauen, Deutsche Treue,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>German women, German loyalty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Deutscher Wein und Deutscher Sang</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>German wine and German song</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Sollen in der Welt behalten</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shall keep in the world</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ihren alten schönen Klang,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Their old, beautiful sound,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Uns zu edler That begeistern</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shall inspire us to noble deeds</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Unser ganzes Leben lang —</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All our life long —</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Deutsche Frauen, Deutsche Treue,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>German women, German loyalty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Deutscher Wein und Deutscher Sang!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>German wine and German song!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Einigkeit und Recht und Freiheit</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Unity and justice and freedom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Für das Deutsche Vaterland!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For the German fatherland!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Danach laßt uns alle streben</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us all strive for that</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Brüderlich mit Herz und Hand!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Like brothers, with heart and hand!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Einigkeit und Recht und Freiheit</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Unity and justice and freedom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Sind des Glückes Unterpfand —</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Are the pledge of happiness —</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Blüh’ im Glanze dieses Glückes,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bloom in the radiance of this happiness,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Blühe Deutsches Vaterland!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bloom, German fatherland!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: August Heinrich Hoffmann von Fallersleben (1841). Text from <a href="https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/Lied_der_Deutschen" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'über Alles' means 'above everything' (to be put before all else), not a claim to rule over other lands.</li><li>line 1.3: 'zu Schutz und Trutze' is a fixed pair: for defence and for resistance (against attack).</li><li>lines 1.5-1.6: Maas = the Meuse (river, west), Memel = the Neman (river, east), Etsch = the Adige (river, south), Belt = the Little Belt (strait, north, in Denmark).</li><li>line 3.1: 'Recht' means both 'right' and 'law'; 'justice' is used here.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="BgDtm5qZKb4" data-title="Deutschlandlied: Dresdner Kreuzchor, the third stanza (2016)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Dresdner Kreuzchor, the third stanza (2016)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel CHOR GESANG - Das Musikmagazin. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BgDtm5qZKb4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sieben Stücke, Op. 145 (Max Reger)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1915–1916</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kinderhymne (Bertolt Brecht, music by Hanns Eisler)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">alternative anthem written as a counterpart</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The End... (Nico)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1974</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording of all three stanzas</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Recording for schools in Baden-Württemberg (Heino)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1977</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording of all three stanzas</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Germania (Laibach, album Volk)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2006</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Afrikaners Landgenote</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, lyrics on the same plan</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser (Joseph Haydn, words by Lorenz Leopold Haschka)</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1797</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li></ul></section>
